      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786852971606,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Independent worker pools with per-tier provider budgets\n- For example, Say Twilio sells us 1,000 SMS per second. Today any tier can spend all 1,000 of\nthem leading to our OTP being starved.\n- carve that allocation up by tier, enforce it with a shared token bucket in REDIS. Every worker\ndraws a token before it calls the provider, and we refill the bucket at the top of each one-second\nwindow under 2 rules:\n1) High priority gets a reserved slice of the bucket. Say, 100 of the 1,000 requests per second\nare allocated just for the high priority group.\n2) Standard gets the remaining 900 and can't touch the reserved 100, no matter how far behind\nit falls.\n\nChallenges:\n- high priority pool can sit underused, but this is deliberated choices.\n- Keep pool ready ensures it's available for OTPs and fraud alerts",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Independent worker pools with per-tier provider budgets\n- For example, Say Twilio sells us 1,000 SMS per second. Today any tier can spend all 1,000 of them leading to our OTP being starved.\n- carve that allocation up by tier, enforce it with a shared token bucket in REDIS. Every worker draws a token before it calls the provider, and we refill the bucket at the top of each one-second window under 2 rules:\n1) High priority gets a reserved slice of the bucket. Say, 100 of the 1,000 requests per second are allocated just for the high priority group.\n2) Standard gets the remaining 900 and can't touch the reserved 100, no matter how far behind it falls.\n\nChallenges:\n- high priority pool can sit underused, but this is deliberated choices.\n- Keep pool ready ensures it's available for OTPs and fraud alerts",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
