    {
      "id": "accyzRawUgfJA7xi_rmnY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1028.743883818829,
      "y": 2523.3785682998514,
      "width": 953.171746465516,
      "height": 475,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bv8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1627859540,
      "version": 307,
      "versionNonce": 1880084180,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1786853843915,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Send-time optimization. We delivered marketing's 9am campaign at 9am. The fancier version\nasks when each user actually engages and picks a per-user send time inside a window marketing\nallows. Our pipeline already handles millions of small scheduled sends, so the interesting part is\ninteracting with an offline ML model that scores those send times. If your interviewer has an ML\nbackground, they'll probably want to talk about this.\n\n\n2) Frequency capping. A real platform eventually caps how many notifications a user gets per day\nacross all types. A good start is next to the preference check at dispatch time, but we need to\ntalk about counters.\n\n3) Campaign cancellation. Marketing schedules a blast, then legal wants it stopped. Or we\nspotted a typo. We expect this to be rare, but serious. How can we check for cancellations\nwithout dragging on performance?\n\n\n4) Timezone-aware delivery. \"9am\" usually means 9am for the user, which means that campaign is\nprobably spread out over 24 hours. The scheduling machinery we already built fits, just with two\ndozen due times instead of one.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Send-time optimization. We delivered marketing's 9am campaign at 9am. The fancier version asks when each user actually engages and picks a per-user send time inside a window marketing allows. Our pipeline already handles millions of small scheduled sends, so the interesting part is interacting with an offline ML model that scores those send times. If your interviewer has an ML background, they'll probably want to talk about this.\n\n\n2) Frequency capping. A real platform eventually caps how many notifications a user gets per day across all types. A good start is next to the preference check at dispatch time, but we need to talk about counters.\n\n3) Campaign cancellation. Marketing schedules a blast, then legal wants it stopped. Or we spotted a typo. We expect this to be rare, but serious. How can we check for cancellations without dragging on performance?\n\n\n4) Timezone-aware delivery. \"9am\" usually means 9am for the user, which means that campaign is probably spread out over 24 hours. The scheduling machinery we already built fits, just with two dozen due times instead of one.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
